  "worked_solution": "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇍\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $2$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $12.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $12$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $3$ and $4.$\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇒\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇐\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇔\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇏\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }8∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=12$ is divisible by $12$ but not divisible by $8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
